    {
      "id": "p1",
      "text": "A single tip from a whistleblower in Sikar delivered precise evidence—PDFs matching the chemistry paper—that exposed a systemic flaw in the medical entrance examination held on May 3. The National Testing Agency, acting as the institutional operator, initiated an investigation by referring 26–27 paper setters and translators to the CBI, a mechanism for systemic correction. This cascade led to the invalidation of the manipulated process, with NEET-UG 2026 cancelled and thirteen people arrested, marking accountability for those who exploited the system.",
      "source": {
        "agent_id": "The-Magician",
        "fragments": [
          "why-0",
          "how-0",
          "how-1",
          "how-2",
          "result-0",
          "result-1"
        ],
        "channel_role": "toned"
      },
      "warnings": [],
      "fit": 0.88
    },
    {
      "id": "p2",
      "text": "The catalyst communication—an email from a tipster containing leaked documents—unmasked the compromised event. This information transfer from source to authority triggered a re-direction of the investigation, revealing leverage points within the system and leading to exposure and removal of manipulators. The outcome was a systemic reset: the exam cancellation and a new examination date set for June 21, offering a second chance for the transformed system.",
      "source": {
        "agent_id": "The-Magician",
        "fragments": [
          "how-1",
          "how-2",
          "how-3",
          "result-0",
          "result-2"
        ],
        "channel_role": "toned"
      },
      "warnings": [],
      "fit": 0.85
    },
    {
      "id": "p3",
      "text": "In this national system undergoing correction, a lever-puller provided proof of systemic information asymmetry. The testing authority escalated the systemic inquiry, leading to arrests made and the rescheduling of the test. The corrective measure following the paradigm disruption—a planned re-examination for June 21—now offers an opportunity for a fair process to be restored.",
